entries.content:
NeoCity Metatypes:
Human (Baseline metatype; the most adaptable and numerous in the world);
Elf (Tall, elegant, and long-lived; often gifted in magic and charisma);
Dwarf (Short, stocky, and resilient; renowned for their toughness and technical skills);
Ork (Strong and hardy; larger than humans and often face prejudice due to stereotypes);
Troll (Massive and physically imposing; natural dermal deposits provide additional armor);
Metavariants:
Night One (Elven variant; marked by dark fur, sharp claws, and heightened agility);
Dryad (Elven variant; closely tied to nature with natural charisma and magical talent);
Koborokuru (Dwarven variant from East Asia; smaller and more reclusive);
Giant (Troll variant; even larger and stronger, but slower and more socially ostracized);
Cyclops (Troll variant; single-eyed and exceptionally strong);
Oni (Ork variant; horned, often red-skinned, with cultural ties to Japanese mythology);
Hobgoblin (Ork variant; leaner and more cunning, primarily found in the Middle East);
Fomori (Troll variant; slightly less physically imposing but more magically resistant);
SURGE Affected:
Changeling (Individuals of any metatype whose appearance and abilities were altered by sudden magical mutations during the Year of the Comet; traits vary widely).

NeoCity Specializations:
Street Samurai (Cyber-enhanced warrior; masters of combat with cyberware and martial skill);
Decker (Cyberspace hacker; uses the Matrix to infiltrate systems and gather intelligence);
Rigger (Vehicle and drone expert; controls drones and vehicles remotely for combat or utility);
Adept (Magically enhanced individual; channels magic into physical abilities like speed and strength);
Mage (Spellcaster; manipulates mana to cast spells for combat, healing, or support);
Shaman (Spirit summoner; draws magical power from totems and communicates with spirits);
Face (Charismatic negotiator; specializes in social manipulation, persuasion, and leadership);
Technomancer (Digital magician; manipulates the Matrix with innate abilities rather than hardware);
Weapon Specialist (Firearms expert; highly skilled with a variety of ranged or melee weapons);
Mercenary (Hired gun; versatile combatant with military or paramilitary training);
Bounty Hunter (Tracker and hunter; skilled in pursuing targets and combat encounters);
Street Doc (Combat medic; skilled in healing and cyberware installation in the field);
Infiltrator (Stealth expert; specializes in sneaking, lockpicking, and avoiding detection);
Drone Wrangler (Advanced Rigger specialization; focuses exclusively on drone operations);
Combat Mage (Magic user specialized in destructive and combat-oriented spells);
Sprawl Rat (Survivalist; street-smart operative skilled in scavenging and urban navigation);
Driver (Vehicle expert; skilled in high-speed chases and evasive maneuvers);
Cyber Assassin (Stealthy, cyber-enhanced killer; specializes in silent takedowns and infiltration);
Smuggler (Logistics expert; moves contraband across borders and hostile territories);
Fixer (Broker and dealmaker; connects runners with jobs, gear, and information).

Crime in NeoCity: Lots in NeoCity; The Obsidian Syndicate (most dangerous); Techno-Syndicate (powerful; specialize in cybercrime, hacking, illegal tech trading); Cybersmugglers Guild (network of smugglers; traffic illegal cyber-enhancements, hacking tools, contraband tech);
Yakuza (Japanese organized crime syndicate; operates in smuggling, gambling, and protection rackets);
The Triads (Chinese criminal organization; focus on drug trafficking, human smuggling, and black market tech);
The Mafia (involved in arms trafficking, extortion, and organized crime);
The Vory V Zakone (Russian crime syndicate; specializes in cybercrime, weapons smuggling, and assassination);
The Black Lodge (secretive magical society involved in high-profile assassinations and occult activities);
The Seoulpa Rings (Korean criminal groups specializing in tech theft, data piracy, and underground cybernetics).
